---
/**
 * The privacy policy (T80.11), linked from the Play listing, rewritten for the public apps (D64):
 * usage analytics recorded on the device always and sent only with consent to the developer's own
 * Worker (PEN-137); local logs that leave only attached to a bug report; the website counts page
 * views with Cloudflare Web Analytics (PEN-123);
 * updates, downloads and Sign in with GitHub go to github.com; search by meaning
 * downloads its model from Hugging Face once; Send feedback uses the visitor's own GitHub token.
 * Keep docs/android.md's Play Data safety answers in step, and change the date with the text.
 */
import SiteLayout from '../components/SiteLayout.astro';
import { REPOSITORY } from '../site';
---

<SiteLayout
  title="Privacy policy · Penmoss"
  description="Penmoss has no account and never receives your notes. Usage data leaves your device only if you agree, and never holds anything you write or name."
>
  <article class="wrap prose">
    <h1>Privacy policy</h1>
    <p class="meta">Effective 19 November 2026</p>

    <p>
      Penmoss is a free, open source notes app for the Mac, Android and the command line. It is made
      by Ryan Henderson, an individual developer, not a company (&ldquo;the developer&rdquo; below).
      &ldquo;You&rdquo; means anyone who uses the apps or this website.
    </p>

    <p>
      <strong>In short.</strong> Penmoss has no account. Your notes stay on your devices and in a git
      repository that you choose and control, and the developer never receives them. The apps keep
      a record of which commands and screens you use, on your device, and send it to the
      developer&rsquo;s own server only if you agree; it never holds anything you write or name.
      There are no ads, and nothing is sold or shared for anyone else&rsquo;s use. This website only
      counts page views, without cookies.
    </p>

    <h2>Where your notes are</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>On your devices.</strong> Each app keeps a copy of your notebooks: notes, folders, tags,
        daily and weekly notes, attached files, version history, comments and settings. It keeps them
        in its own private storage, so they open offline.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>In your git repository.</strong> Each notebook is a git repository (or a folder in one)
        that you add yourself. The apps sync with it using your git host&rsquo;s own sign-in: a token
        you give, your Mac&rsquo;s git credentials, or Sign in with GitHub. Your git host stores the
        notes under its own terms and privacy policy. Penmoss does not check whether the repository
        is private: if you choose a public repository, anyone can read your notes there.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Version history</strong> is the repository&rsquo;s commit history. Each change records
        which device or agent made it, with the commit name and email you set.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Penmoss is not end-to-end encrypted: your notes are stored in your repository as files your git
      host can read.
    </p>

    <h2>What leaves your device</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Sync</strong> goes only to the git repositories you added, over HTTPS or SSH.</li>
      <li>
        <strong>GitHub.</strong> When you add a notebook on the Mac, the app asks GitHub which repositories
        you can reach, and can create one for you. Sign in with GitHub talks to github.com directly.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Updates and downloads.</strong> The Mac app checks for updates on GitHub Releases, and
        the download links on this website go to GitHub. GitHub sees your IP address, under its own
        terms.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Semantic search</strong> on the Mac downloads its model once from Hugging Face. After
        that it runs on your Mac: no note text leaves it to search.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Send feedback</strong>, when you choose to use it, files an issue in <a
          href={REPOSITORY}>Penmoss&rsquo;s public GitHub repository</a
        > with your own GitHub token. The issue holds what you write, the screenshots you attach and
        a short description of the screen and app version, but never note text unless you put it
        there. It is public, under your GitHub account, and GitHub stores it under its own terms.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>AI agents you connect</strong>, such as Claude Code through Penmoss&rsquo;s command-line
        tool and local MCP server, can read and change the notebook you connect them to. They run on
        your Mac, and their providers handle what they read under their own terms. Penmoss never
        does this on its own.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Pictures from other websites.</strong> When a note shows a picture by its web address,
        your device downloads it from that website, which sees your IP address. On Android you can turn
        this off in Settings.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The apps send no crash reports by themselves. Usage data and logs are explained below. This
      website counts page views with Cloudflare Web Analytics, which totals the pages opened,
      the sites visitors came from, their browsers and countries, sets no cookies, and gets nothing
      from the apps; it is the only thing the website loads from another site.
    </p>

    <h2>Usage data</h2>
    <p>
      <strong>Recorded on your device, always.</strong> From the first launch, the Mac app, the
      <code>penmoss</code> command-line tool, its local MCP server and the Android app record what you
      do in Penmoss, by name: which commands, shortcuts, buttons, menus, screens, dialogs and settings
      you use, which CLI commands run and which MCP tools agents call, whether a person, an agent or a
      script did it, the app&rsquo;s version, the system&rsquo;s version, the window&rsquo;s size class, the
      window&rsquo;s size and layout (its size to the nearest 10 pixels, and its sidebar, note panel, Full
      Width, light or dark and how many tabs, never where it is or which screen it is on), how far you
      scroll the note, the sidebar and Settings (from top to bottom in hundredths, never what is there),
      and a random analytics ID. Counts are rounded into ranges and times into durations. The record stays
      on the device for 7 days (at most 5,000 events) unless you change that, between 1 and 360 days,
      in Settings &rarr; Privacy &rarr; Advanced or with <code>penmoss analytics limits</code>. You can
      read all of it in Settings &rarr; Privacy &rarr; View Analytics Data, or with
      <code>penmoss analytics show</code>.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Sent only with your consent.</strong> At the end of setting up, or once after the update
      that added it, Penmoss asks whether to share it. If you choose Share Usage Data, the record,
      setup included, is sent to the developer&rsquo;s own server at t.penmoss.app, which runs on
      Cloudflare for the developer. It keeps the events for 13 months and no IP address, and only the
      developer reads them, through a private dashboard, to learn how Penmoss is used. If you choose
      Don&rsquo;t Share, nothing is ever sent. The command-line tool and the MCP server follow the
      app&rsquo;s answer and never send anything themselves; <code>DO_NOT_TRACK=1</code> or
      <code>PENMOSS_ANALYTICS=off</code> keeps their record on the device.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Never recorded:</strong> note titles, text or excerpts; file, folder, tag, attachment,
      notebook or repository names, paths, branches and links; search text; GitHub usernames, email
      addresses, tokens and keys; command-line arguments; what agents ask for or read; anything you
      type.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Your choices.</strong> In Settings &rarr; Privacy, on the Mac and Android, or with
      <code>penmoss analytics</code>: turn sharing off (anything not yet sent stays on your device);
      reset your analytics ID; Delete My Shared Data, which deletes everything your device shared from
      the developer&rsquo;s server and gives it a new ID; and change how much is kept.
    </p>

    <h2>Logs</h2>
    <p>
      The Mac and Android apps keep a log on the device (when the app starts, windows open, sync runs,
      updates are checked, and errors), with tokens, email addresses, repository addresses, your
      GitHub login, notebook names, paths, IDs and quoted text replaced as each line is written. The
      Mac keeps it in <code>~/Library/Logs/Penmoss</code>, 2 files of 1 MB for 7 days by default, which
      you can change in Settings &rarr; Privacy &rarr; Advanced. The logs leave your device only if you
      attach them to a bug report in Send Feedback, after a preview that shows exactly what is sent,
      or, on Android, if you share them with Share Log File.
    </p>

    <h2>The developer&rsquo;s own relay</h2>
    <p>
      The developer runs one small service on Cloudflare, the relay, so that Claude on the web and on
      mobile can reach the developer&rsquo;s own notebook. It signs in only the developer&rsquo;s
      GitHub account and serves only that one repository. It never handles anyone else&rsquo;s
      notes.
    </p>

    <h2>What the apps keep on your devices</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Mac:</strong> your notebooks in the app&rsquo;s storage; the tokens you give, encrypted
        with a key in the macOS Keychain; the semantic search model and its index, your usage record
        and your sharing answer, in <code>~/.penmoss</code>; and the app&rsquo;s log, in
        <code>~/Library/Logs/Penmoss</code>.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Android:</strong> your notebooks, attached files and downloaded web pictures in the
        app&rsquo;s private storage, your usage record, sharing answer and log, and your tokens encrypted
        with a key held in the Android Keystore.
        None of it goes into Android&rsquo;s cloud backup or device-to-device transfer. When the app
        asks for your fingerprint, face or screen lock before a change, Android checks it on the phone;
        Penmoss never receives it.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Command line:</strong> its notebooks under <code>~/.penmoss</code>, and its tokens in the
        system keychain or a private settings file.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>Removing an app removes these copies from that device. Your repository keeps the notes.</p>
    <p>
      Shared usage data is deleted from the developer&rsquo;s server after 13 months, or at once when
      you choose Delete My Shared Data in Settings &rarr; Privacy or run
      <code>penmoss analytics forget</code>.
    </p>

    <h2>Deleting your data</h2>
    <p>
      Deleting a note moves it to Trash. Delete for good removes it from your notebook. Earlier
      versions stay in the repository&rsquo;s history until you rewrite or delete the repository.
      Because the repository is yours, you can delete it, or anything in it, at any time with your git
      host.
    </p>

    <h2>Security</h2>
    <p>
      Every connection uses HTTPS or SSH. On the Mac and Android, deleting for good and other
      sensitive changes need Touch ID, a fingerprint or the device&rsquo;s password first. Your git
      host decides how your repository is protected at rest. To report a security problem, see <a
        href={`${REPOSITORY}/security/policy`}>the security policy</a
      >.
    </p>

    <h2>Children</h2>
    <p>
      Penmoss is not meant for anyone under 18 and does not knowingly collect data from children.
    </p>

    <h2>Changes to this policy</h2>
    <p>
      If the way Penmoss handles data changes, this page is updated first and its effective date
      changes with it. Its history is in <a href={`${REPOSITORY}/commits/main/site/src/pages/privacy.astro`}
        >the repository</a
      >.
    </p>

    <h2>Contact</h2>
    <p>
      For questions, open an issue on <a href={`${REPOSITORY}/issues`}>GitHub</a>, or use the developer
      contact email on Penmoss&rsquo;s Google Play listing.
    </p>
  </article>
</SiteLayout>
